# Passive Tracer Workbook

An example of the passive transport solver I have currently.
SIMPLE is not far away as my abstractions are very extensible
and it shouldn't be so hard to implement 

## Meshing

Currently the meshing capabilities is very limited but this will be extended
with an expressive toolkit for defining parametric 2D geometries.  Though
for now we'll stick to a uniform cartesian mesh for simplicity.

j(define mesh
  (structured-mesh :nx 50 :ny 25 :width 1 :height 0.5))

Notice the keyword arguments (`kwargs`) which start with a colon.  These aren't
strictly a feature of Scheme, the lisp I am mostly basing this dialect on,
but for a config DSL they seemed like a really nice bit of syntactic sugar.

## Fields

A field (when resolved) is just a container for cell data like cellValues,
cellValues0 (values from previous timestep) and faceValues.

j(define dye-field
  (prognostic-scalar-field "dye" :initial-value 0))

Until I get a SIMPLE solver fully working, I'm using a derived vector field
that takes a callback function to calculate it's uniform value every timestep.
I'm going to make it sinusoidal so I'll import my maths library.

(import "maths")

j(define x-velocity 0.03)

j(define (y-velocity time)
  (* 0.01 (maths/sin time)))

j(define velocity-field
  (derived-vector-field "velocity"
    (lambda (time) [x-velocity (y-velocity time)])))

## Equations

An equation is the orchestrator for assembling the system(s) of linear equations
that get solved every iteration.  The basic flow is it takes an arbritrary
amount of operators (allowing for lovely mathematical expressiveness and
experimentation), and then at resolution it sorts them into `fluxOps` and
`srcOps` whereby:

- **Flux Operators**: calculate a "flux" for every *connection* in the mesh, eg
  for basic heat diffusion the laplacian operator will calculate
  `k * A / d`. 

- **Source Operators**: calculate a source term for the rhs vector and some
  diagonal contribution for the matrix for every *cell* in the mesh.  For
  example a point source just adds some constant to the rhs, but the
  unsteady operator (`ddt`) adds `a_P^0 / dt` to the rhs and `a_P^0` to
  the diagonal.

j(define density 1) ;; A dye is just concentration so density=1

j(define diffusivity 1e-6) ;; To keep the Peclet number high

j(define dye-equation
  (equation dye-field
    (ddt dye-field density)
    (div dye-field density velocity-field)
    (laplacian dye-field diffusivity)
    (scalar-point-src [-1 0] 0.1)))

Note the point source coordinates are in [-1, 1] space rather than the actual
physics space for consistency.

### Boundary conditions

The equation is responsible for boundary conditions rather than the field,
as I want to keep the `field` data type as dumb as possible.  Boundary conditions
are applied with the source terms as "decorators" of the matrix and rhs, using
cached flux values from fluxOps corresponding to boundary connections.

j(set-boundary-conditions dye-equation mesh
  :north-border (scalar-neumann 0)
  :east-border (scalar-outflow)
  :south-border (scalar-neumann 0)
  :west-border (scalar-dirichlet 0))

## Solver

This is pretty self explanatory, the solver used by a scenario. I've implemented
the CG method with a basic Jacobi preconditioner as a sweeet spot for simplicity
and performance on the browser, however for more advanced use cases of running
many convergence cases back-to-back I'd implement a more complicated
multi-threaded solver and then serialise the problem definition (ie the 
workbook) and send it from the browser to a server somewhere to run it at full
speed.  For small visualisations on the browser though CG seems pretty good.

j(define solver 
  (jacobi-preconditioned-cg :max-iterations 100 :tolerance 1e-3))

## Scenario

A `Scenario` is the main orchestrator of fields and equations, and is responsible
for orchestrating an iteration step and relaying if it's reached convergence
for a given time step.  For a pressure-velocity solver this'll involved multiple
iterations and lots of complicated bits like organising Rhie-Chow MWI for the
velocity face values and caching that in an iteration etc.  For my basic
passive transport case I need none of that - I just assemble equations and solve
and assume convergence in one step.

Furthermore, a `Scenario` is responsible for the complete "resolution" of the
problem - the data structures declared earlier (meshes, fields, equations) are
very sparse definition types under the hood that do not have any of the long
data arrays initialised yet, to avoid allocating memory while defining the
problem.  When it comes time to run a visualisation or run a scenario to
convergence the scenario will resolve all its components and only then will
the long data arrays be initialised.

j(passive-transport
  :mesh mesh
  :solver solver
  :fields [dye-field velocity-field]
  :equations [dye-equation])

And there you have it - a complete definition for a scenario that can be
run as a visualisation.  Under the hood the interpreter for this DSL
lexes and parses the file, complaining if there are any syntax errors,
and then evaluates every top-level expression (ie the ones starting with
the `j` glyph) and responds with a json list of every block, it's location
in the code and some metadata about the thing it has evaluated to.
My web-interface can read this and knows that if it's resolved to a
MeshDefinition or a ScenarioDefinition to offer UI to visualise them
and then to interact with the solver code to do that.
